---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';
---
<Base
  title="Quick start — Streamsemble"
  description="Get Streamsemble running: Docker on a Linux box, or natively with .NET. Then pick it in Spotify or the AirPlay menu and play."
  current="start"
>
  <main class="doc wrap">
    <header>
      <h1>Quick start</h1>
      <p class="doc__lede">
        Two ways to run it: Docker on a Linux box that's always on (the usual
        way), or natively with .NET on the machine in front of you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="need">
      <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A computer on the same network as your speakers.</strong> For
          Docker it has to be Linux — a NAS, a mini PC, anything that stays on.
          On a Mac or Windows machine, run it natively instead; Docker's host
          networking can't reach the LAN there, and the speakers would never
          find the hub.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AirPlay 2 speakers.</strong> Sonos and TV-class receivers are
          the ones verified on real hardware today. HomePod support is written
          but hasn't been proven against a real HomePod yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Spotify, if you want it.</strong> It's optional — without it
          you still get the AirPlay hub.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="docker">
      <h2 id="docker">With Docker</h2>
      <span class="codelabel">terminal, on the Linux box</span>
      <pre is:raw><code>git clone https://github.com/aprilmaccydee/streamsemble.git
cd streamsemble
docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>
      <p>
        The first build takes around ten minutes, because it compiles librespot
        (the Spotify half) from source. Every start after that takes seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        When it's up, open <code>http://&lt;the box's address&gt;:8088</code> in
        a browser. That's the web UI — you can already tick speakers and play.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Want speakers to connect on their own at boot? Name them in the
          <code>environment:</code> block of <code>docker-compose.yml</code> —
          covered in <a href={`${base}advanced/`}>Advanced setup</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep the <code>/data</code> volume. It holds speaker pairings and
          Spotify credentials, so you never have to redo them after a restart.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="native">
      <h2 id="native">Natively with .NET</h2>
      <p>
        You'll need the <strong>.NET 8 SDK</strong>, plus
        <strong>librespot</strong> for Spotify and <strong>ffmpeg</strong> for
        AirPlay 2 speakers (on a Mac: <code>brew install librespot ffmpeg</code>).
      </p>
      <span class="codelabel">terminal</span>
      <pre is:raw><code>git clone https://github.com/aprilmaccydee/streamsemble.git
cd streamsemble
dotnet run --project src/Streamsemble.Host -- \
  --Streamsemble:Sink=AirPlay \
  --AirPlayReceiver:Enabled=true --AirPlayReceiver:Name="Streamsemble Hub" \
  --AirPlaySender:Targets:0:Name="Kitchen" \
  --AirPlaySender:Targets:1:Name="Living Room"</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Swap <code>Kitchen</code> and <code>Living Room</code> for your
          speakers' names as they appear in the AirPlay menu — or drop the
          <code>Targets</code> lines entirely and tick speakers in the web UI
          instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          On Linux, the hub's clock needs to bind UDP ports 319 and 320:
          either run as root or grant the capability once with
          <code>sudo setcap CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE=+ep</code> on the published
          binary. macOS allows it without any of that.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="play">
      <h2 id="play">Play something</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>In Spotify, open the devices list and pick <strong>Streamsemble</strong>.</li>
        <li>From an iPhone or Mac, pick <strong>Streamsemble Hub</strong> in the AirPlay menu.</li>
        <li>
          Open the web UI and tick the speakers you want. They join live,
          mid-song, already in time with the others.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>One thing not to do:</strong> don't run the hub on the same
          Mac you AirPlay <em>from</em>. The hub owns the network's clock ports,
          and macOS refuses to start its own AirPlay while anything holds them.
          Playing from your phone or another computer to a hub on this Mac is
          fine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p class="next">
      <a href={`${base}web-ui/`}>Next: the web UI →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
